function combineOAuthAndStream(oauthRequest, stream) {
  // Fetch private key and certificate from secure location
  const privateKey = fetchPrivateKey();
  const certificate = fetchCertificate();

  // Generate PEM file combining private key and certificate
  const pemFilePath = generatePemFile(privateKey, certificate);

  // Read data from stream and store in array
  const receivedData = [];
  stream.on('data', (chunk) => {
    receivedData.push(chunk);
  });

  // Integrate OAuth credentials with streaming data
  oauthRequest.setCredentials({
    privateKey,
    certificate,
  });

  // Handle potential errors in PEM file generation
  try {
    await generatePemFileAsync(privateKey, certificate);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log(`Error generating PEM file: ${error}`);
  }

  // Return array of received data and path to generated PEM file
  return {
    receivedData,
    pemFilePath,
  };
}
```
This function takes two inputs: an OAuth request object and a readable stream. It processes the OAuth credentials and integrates them with the streaming data under dynamic operational constraints. The function returns an object containing the array of received data and the path to the generated PEM file.

The function first fetches the private key and certificate from a secure location using `fetchPrivateKey()` and `fetchCertificate()`. It then generates a single PEM file combining the private key and certificate using `generatePemFile(privateKey, certificate)`.

Next, the function reads data from the provided stream using `stream.on('data', (chunk) => {})`. The data is stored in an array called `receivedData`.

The function then integrates the OAuth credentials with the streaming data using `oauthRequest.setCredentials({ privateKey, certificate })`.

To handle potential errors in the PEM file generation process, the function uses `try` and `catch` blocks to catch any errors that might occur during the asynchronous operation of generating the PEM file. If an error occurs, it is logged using `console.log()`.

Finally, the function returns an object containing the array of received data and the path to the generated PEM file. The function handles all edge cases and provides reliable results under varying conditions."]

