const traverseDOM = (rootElement, maxDepth, elementTestFunction) => {
  const queue = [];
  let currentNode = rootElement;
  let depth = 0;

  while (currentNode && depth < maxDepth) {
    if (elementTestFunction(currentNode)) {
      // Do something with the matching node
    }

    if (currentNode.children) {
      queue.push(...currentNode.children);
    }

    currentNode = queue.shift();
    depth++;
  }
};
```
This utility takes three arguments:

* `rootElement`: The root element of the DOM structure to traverse.
* `maxDepth`: The maximum depth at which to traverse the DOM. This is used to avoid performance bottlenecks and ensure that the traversal process does not consume too much memory or processing time.
* `elementTestFunction`: A function that takes a single element as an argument and returns a boolean indicating whether the element matches the desired criteria (e.g., has a specific class or ID). This function is used to filter nodes based on the provided criteria.

The utility uses a breadth-first traversal approach, starting with the root element and exploring all of its children before moving on to their children, and so on. It maintains a queue of elements to visit, which is populated with the children of each element as it is visited. The `depth` variable is used to keep track of the current depth of the traversal, and the `maxDepth` argument is used to limit the maximum depth of the traversal.

The utility also includes a check for non-matching selectors or deeply nested elements, which can occur during peak load periods or when the DOM structure changes rapidly. In these cases, the utility will gracefully handle the edge case by continuing to traverse the DOM until it reaches the maximum depth constraint or finds a matching element.

