The function first allocates memory for the PDUs using the `alloc_LTE_PHY_PDU` function, which checks if there are any available blocks in the cache before allocating a new block from the heap. The allocated memory is then initialized using the `init_PHY_TXDU` and `init_PHY_RXDU` functions.

The function then handles concurrent access from multiple threads by acquiring a mutex lock before performing operations on the PDUs. This ensures that only one thread can access the PDUs at a time, preventing race conditions and data corruption.

After performing operations on the PDUs, the function deallocates the memory using the `free_LTE_PHY_PDU` function, which checks if the block is in the cache before deallocating it from the heap. This helps to reduce memory overhead and optimize for both worst-case and average-case scenarios.

The function also includes error handling for allocation failures, which can occur when there are no available blocks in the cache or when the requested size exceeds the maximum allowed size. In such cases, the function exits with an error message to prevent a system-wide failure.

